{"name":"Citra","hex":"#f6b906","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"citra","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of chitra (“Indian art genre”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/citra"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chitra (art)","blocks":[{"text":"Chitra or citra is an Indian genre of art that includes painting, sketch and any art form of delineation. The earliest mention of the term Chitra in the context of painting or picture is found in some of the ancient Sanskrit texts of Hinduism and Pali texts of Buddhism."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Exposition_Clemenceau%2C_le_Tigre_et_l%27Asie_%28MNAA-Guimet%2C_Paris%29_%2813888446659%29.jpg/330px-Exposition_Clemenceau%2C_le_Tigre_et_l%27Asie_%28MNAA-Guimet%2C_Paris%29_%2813888446659%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chitra_(art)"},{"title":"Citra (drink)","blocks":[{"text":"Citra was a clear lemon- and lime-flavoured soda sold in India in the 1980s and early 1990s. Citra was owned by Parle Bisleri. Along with other Parle brands, Thums Up, Limca, Gold Spot and Maaza, Citra was sold to Coca-Cola in 1993 in a deal that was reportedly worth $40 million. At the time of sale, the Parle brands together had a 60% market share in the aerated water industry. The brand was strong in South India. Citra was phased out by the year 2000 to make way for Coke's international brand, Sprite.\nMarketed as a \"super cooler\" targeting young consumers, Citra was \npositioned as a clear alternative to the cloudy Limca, more \nclosely resembling traditional nimbu paani."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/45/CitraIndia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Citra_(drink)"},{"title":"Citra (emulator)","blocks":[{"text":"Citra is a discontinued free and open-source game console emulator of the Nintendo 3DS, a handheld system, for Windows, macOS, Linux, and Android. Citra's name is derived from CTR, which is the model name of the original 3DS. Citra can run many homebrew games and commercial games."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Citra_2104_Screenshot_with_About_dialog.png/330px-Citra_2104_Screenshot_with_About_dialog.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Citra_(emulator)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Chola_fresco.png/500px-Chola_fresco.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Chola_fresco.png/500px-Chola_fresco.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Chola era mural at a Hindu temple.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChola_fresco.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/1780_CE_Rama_and_Sita_in_forest%2C_Lakshmana_removing_thorn.jpg/500px-1780_CE_Rama_and_Sita_in_forest%2C_Lakshmana_removing_thorn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/1780_CE_Rama_and_Sita_in_forest%2C_Lakshmana_removing_thorn.jpg/500px-1780_CE_Rama_and_Sita_in_forest%2C_Lakshmana_removing_thorn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 1780 CE painting of a Ramayana scene in Kangra-school style; Rama and Sita in forest, Lakshmana removing thorn","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1780_CE_Rama_and_Sita_in_forest%2C_Lakshmana_removing_thorn.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/16th_century_Virabhadra_temple%2C_Lepakshi%2C_Vijayanagara_era_Murals.jpg/500px-16th_century_Virabhadra_temple%2C_Lepakshi%2C_Vijayanagara_era_Murals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/16th_century_Virabhadra_temple%2C_Lepakshi%2C_Vijayanagara_era_Murals.jpg/500px-16th_century_Virabhadra_temple%2C_Lepakshi%2C_Vijayanagara_era_Murals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Some of the better preserved Vijayanagara-era chitra murals are in Virabhadra temple, Lepakshi, Andhra Pradesh. These include secular and religious paintings. Above: Parvati getting ready for her wedding to Shiva; the mandapa with painted ceiling in the background.[35]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A16th_century_Virabhadra_temple%2C_Lepakshi%2C_Vijayanagara_era_Murals.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Female_Ascetics_%28Yoginis%29_LACMA_M.2011.156.4_%281_of_2%29.jpg/500px-Female_Ascetics_%28Yoginis%29_LACMA_M.2011.156.4_%281_of_2%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Female_Ascetics_%28Yoginis%29_LACMA_M.2011.156.4_%281_of_2%29.jpg/1280px-Female_Ascetics_%28Yoginis%29_LACMA_M.2011.156.4_%281_of_2%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Female_Ascetics_%28Yoginis%29_LACMA_M.2011.156.4_%281_of_2%29.jpg/500px-Female_Ascetics_%28Yoginis%29_LACMA_M.2011.156.4_%281_of_2%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Yoginis, Rajput-school of chitrakala","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFemale_Ascetics_(Yoginis)_LACMA_M.2011.156.4_(1_of_2).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/KALAMKARI_HAND_PAINTED_CLOTH_SRIKALAHASTI_AP_-_panoramio.jpg/500px-KALAMKARI_HAND_PAINTED_CLOTH_SRIKALAHASTI_AP_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https: